===Etymology 3===
{{etymid|ja|hiedori}}
{{ja-kanjitab|ひえどり|yomi=kun}}

Alternative name for [[鵯#Japanese:_hiyodori|''hiyodori'']].<ref name="NKDc"/><ref name="DDJS"/><ref name="DJR"/><ref name="NJ"/>  Two derivations are discussed:
* May be a sound shift from [[鵯#Japanese:_hiyodori|''hiyodori'']].<ref name="NJ"/>
* May be a compound of {{compound|ja|稗|tr1=hie|t1=millet|鳥|tr2=tori|t2=bird}}, from the way the bird might eat millet.<ref name="NJ"/>

One reference claims that the brown-eared bulbul does not eat millet.<ref name="NJ"/>  However, other sources indicate that this bird does eat seeds, potentially including millet.<ref>{{cite-journal|author={{w|Mark Brazil}}|url=http://www.japantimes.co.jp/life/2014/03/08/environment/noisy-bulbuls-change-with-the-seasons/|title=Noisy bulbuls change with the seasons|date=March 8, 2016|newspaper={{w|The Japan Times}}|accessdate=13 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|url=http://www.bird-research.jp/1_shiryo/seitai/hiyo.pdf|author=Japan Bird Research Association|title=Brown-eared Bulbul|accessdate=13 April 2026}}</ref>

First attested in the {{m|ja||{{w|本草和名}}|tr=Honzō Wamyō|lit=Japanese names of the true herbs}}, a medicinal reference work from 918 {{CE}}.<ref name="NKDc"/><ref name="DDJS"/>  This reading appears to be less common in modern usage, and dictionaries may not include a full entry for this.<ref name="DJR"/><ref name="SMK5"/>

