===Etymology 3===
{{etymid|ja|kutakake}}
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|くたかけ|clearright=y}}

First attested in {{w|The Tales of Ise}} of the early 900s. Possibly influenced by {{bor|ja|ain|コタン}}.

<div style="width: 75%">{{box|title=Etymological details|
Listed in various references as a compound of {{com|ja|くた|tr1=kuta/kuda|鶏|tr2=kake|t2=chicken; rooster}}.<ref name="NKDc">{{R:ja:NKD Concise}}</ref><ref name="DDJS">{{R:Digital-Daijisen}}</ref><ref name="DJR"/>  This word has also been spelled as {{m|ja|くだかけ|tr=kudakake}}. 

The source of the initial ''kuta-''/''kuda-'' portion has been subject to debate. Several sources have attempted to give an etymology:<ref name="KDJ2O">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2 Online|くた‐かけ 【鶏】|lid=2002013dcacan3Eom4Ko|kotobank=鶏}}</ref>
# The ''{{w|Hikobae}}'' of 1847 and the ''{{w|言海#大言海|lang=ja|Daigenkai}}'' derive ''kuta-'' as a word meaning "stinky", the root of {{m|ja|腐す|tr=kutasu||to rot, to spoil|pos=transitive}} and related to {{m|ja|朽ちる|tr=kuchiru||to rot, to spoil|pos=intransitive}}.
# The ''{{w|Kita-no-bezuihitsu}}'', ''{{w|Meigentsū}}''{{,}} and the ''{{w|Minakata-Kumakusu}}'' derive this from {{m|ja|百済鶏|tr=kudara-kake|lit=Baekje chicken}}, saying that the chicken was imported from there.
# The ''Wakun-no-Shiori'' derives ''kuta'' as an eastern word for "house". The same source also supposes a Sanskrit origin, transcribed as ''kukutaeshira'', and also supposes it was derived from {{m|ja|管掛|tr=kuda-kake}} from the way it has a good voice.
# The ''{{w|Honchō Jigen}}'' derives this from ''kudo-kake'', where ''kudo-'' means "a passing sound" and ''-kake'' means "to put on".
# The ''{{w|Myōgoki}}'' derives ''kuda-'' from {{m|ja|数連|tr=kazutsura}}<!-- What does this mean?  Not finding this in JA resources... -->, while ''-kake'' is an onomatopoeic sound.

The "house" etymology seems the most likely, as this note appears in several manuscripts of ''{{w|The Tales of Ise}}'', and Vovin (2021) identifies ''kuda-'' with {{der|ja|ain|コタン|tr=kotan|t=village|sort=くたかけ}} (cf. {{m+|en|village|w=1}} and {{m|en|villa}}). He also identifies the poem in ''The Tales of Ise'' as being a hybrid Japanese-Ainu poem.<ref name="AinuJ">{{cite-journal|author=Vovin, Alexander|title=Ainu elements in early Japonic|journal=Handbook of the Ainu Language|doi=https://doi.org/10.1515/9781501502859-007|year=2021}}</ref>
}}</div>

