===Etymology 1===
{{align|right|
{{wp|ja:ツル}}
[[File:Grus_japonensis_in_flight_at_Akan_International_Crane_Center.jpg|thumb|250px|{{m|ja|鶴|tr=tsuru, tazu}}: the [[Japanese crane]], ''[[Grus japonensis]]'']]
{{Commonscat|Gruidae}}
[[File:Origami-crane.jpg|thumb|250px|{{m|ja|鶴|tr=tsuru}}: an [[origami]] '''crane''']]
{{Commonscat|Origami crane}}
[[File:Japanese_crest_Tsuru_no_Maru.svg|thumb|250px|{{m|ja|鶴|tr=tsuru}}: the ''{{ll|ja|鶴の丸|tsuru no maru}}'', one of many {{m|ja|家紋|tr=kamon||[[family]] [[crest]]s}} in the depiction of a '''crane''']]
{{Commonscat|Tsuru mon (Japanese crests of crane)}}
}}
{{ja-kanjitab|つる|yomi=k}}
Given a crane's long neck and how the bird hunts, possibly related to {{ja-r|蔓|つる|[[vine]]}}; {{ja-r|弦|つる|[[bowstring]]; [[musical instrument]] [[string]]}}; {{ja-r|釣る|つる|rom=-}}, {{ja-r|吊る|つる|to [[hang]] [[down]]; to [[string up]]; to [[fish#Verb|fish]]}}. Given how cranes flock together, possibly related also to {{ja-r|連る|つる}}, {{w|Classical Japanese|classical}} form of modern verb {{ja-r|連れる|つれる|to [[accompany]]}}. That said, {{ja-r|連る|つる|linkto=-|rom=-}} also appears to ultimately derive from {{ja-r|蔓|つる|[[vine]]}}.

Vovin (2008) considers it possibly related to an ancestor of {{cog|ko|두루미||[[crane]]}}, with a root-final ''-m'' vanishing later, leaving only a [[w:Kansai dialect|Kansai]] accent pattern behind.<ref>{{cite-book|title=Proto-Japanese: Issues and Prospects|editors=Bjarke Frellesvig and John Whitman|year=2008|chapter=7|section=Proto-Japanese beyond the accent system|publisher=John Benjamins Publishing Company|location=Amsterdam; Philadelphia|pages=140-156|chapterurl=https://www.academia.edu/19253123/Proto-Japanese_beyond_the_accent_system}}</ref> May ultimately be a regional [[Wanderwort]]. Compare also {{cog|fiu-pro|*tërka||crane}}, and {{cog|trk-pro|*turńa||crane}} (whence {{cog|tr|turna}}), {{cog|mn|тогоруу||crane}}. {{rfv-etym|ja}}

The reading ''tsuru'' is first seen used to mean “a crane” from the late [[w:Heian period|Heian period]]. Prior to that time, the only reading used for the bird was ''tazu''. However, the kanji {{m|ja||鶴}} was used in the ''[[w:Man'yōshū|''Man'yōshū'']]'' (759 {{CE}}) as a {{ja-r|借%訓|しゃっ%くん}} reading for {{m|ojp||つる|tr=turu → tsuru}}, the {{ja-r|連%体%形|れん%たい%けい|[[attributive]] form}} of {{m|ojp|つ|tr=tu → tsu||pos=[[auxiliary verb]] of [[affirmation]], [[certainty]]{{,}} or [[completion]]}}, suggesting that ''tsuru'' may have already existed as an everyday term meaning “a crane”.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref>

